range 0–75 IU/mL). The echocardiogram showed minimal
mitral regurgitation in the absence of heart murmur; an EKG
was normal. Brain MRI and EEG were also normal.
Sydenham’s chorea was diagnosed, and oral therapy with
Haloperidol was given initially at 0.5 mg and progressively
increased up to 3.4 mg (0.06 mg/kg/die). Prophylaxis with
i.m. penicilline (1,200,000 IU every 3 weeks) was also
started. During hospitalization clinical conditions improved;
choreiform movements, dysarthria, and gait abnormalities
became less prominent, and the patient was therefore dis-
charged on haloperidol, adalimumab, and oral prednisone, as
well as penicillin prophylaxis. After 9 months of follow-up
her choreic movements had disappeared, though some minor
distal twitching could still be detected (Supporting Informa-
tion Video, Segment 2).
The occurrence of chorea in JIA is a rare event, and we
cannot fully explain this unusual association. However, a
common autoimmune background as well as the TNF-block-
ing treatment regimen might all have played a role.
